== Women ==
[[Mary, Queen of Scots]] popularly became the first known woman golfer, when she was accused of playing golf near [[Seton Palace]] only a few days after [[Murder of Lord Darnley|Lord Darnley's murder]], in 1567,<ref>{{cite Britannica |title=Golf > Golf in Scotland |url=https://www.britannica.com/sports/golf#ref222219 |language=en}}</ref> though certain sources claim that this was merely a rumour.<ref>{{cite web |title=Early moments in Scottish golf |url=https://www.nls.uk/collections/stories/sports-and-recreation/early-moments-in-scottish-golf/ |website=National Library of Scotland |access-date=16 February 2026 |archive-date=15 February 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260215232812/https://www.nls.uk/collections/stories/sports-and-recreation/early-moments-in-scottish-golf/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

Other reported early women golfers include two married women who, according to the ''[[Caledonian Mercury]]'' in 1738, played a match on Bruntsfield Links in Edinburgh, with their husbands acting as caddies.<ref>{{cite book |author=Stephen Proctor |title=Matchless: Joyce Wethered, Glenna Collett and the Rise of Women's Golf |publisher=Arena Sport (Birlinn Limited) |location=Edinburgh |year=2025 |isbn=978-1-788857-96-3 |chapter=2 |page=12 |url=https://content.e-bookshelf.de/media/reading/L-27103776-b1ee7c1bd8.pdf}}</ref><ref name="skillen-clothing" />

In the late nineteenth century, women such as [[Issette Pearson]] (a founder of the [[Ladies' Golf Union]]) and [[Lady Margaret Scott (golfer)|Lady Margaret Scott]] (winner of the first [[The Women's Amateur Championship|Ladies Amateur Golf Championship]] in 1893) were prominent early figures in organised women's golf.<ref name="skillen-clothing">{{cite journal |last1=Skillen |first1=Fiona |last2=Beatty |first2=Lauren |title=Conventional to comfortable or respectable to practical: the evolution of women's golf clothing in Britain, 1890–1935 |journal=Costume |volume=56 |issue=2 |year=2022 |pages=208–230 |doi=10.3366/cost.2022.0231 |url=https://researchonline.gcu.ac.uk/en/publications/59cd213d-4708-486a-816e-3fffd51a5590 }}</ref>

[[St Andrews Links]], Scotland is recognised as hosting the first fully constituted ladies' golf club, since 1867, with one in Devon, England following soon after.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Vamplew |first1=Wray |title=Sharing Space: Inclusion, Exclusion, and Accommodation at the British Golf Club Before 1914 |journal=Journal of Sport and Social Issues |date=August 2010 |volume=34 |issue=3 |pages=359–375 |doi=10.1177/0193723510377327}}</ref>

In 1891, the newly built [[Shinnecock Hills Golf Club|Shinnecock Hills]] course in [[Southampton, New York]] became the first US club to offer membership to women golfers.<ref>{{cite web |author1=Julia Pine |title=Shinnecock Hills to Host U.S. Open, U.S. Women's Open in 2036 |url=https://www.usga.org/content/usga/home-page/articles/2024/03/shinnecock-hills-host-us-open-us-womens-open-2036.html |website=USGA |access-date=17 February 2026 |archive-date=15 December 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251215200653/https://www.usga.org/content/usga/home-page/articles/2024/03/shinnecock-hills-host-us-open-us-womens-open-2036.html |url-status=live }}</ref> Four years later, in 1895, The U.S. Golf Association held the first Women's Amateur Championship tournament at the [[Meadow Brook Golf Club]].<ref>{{cite web |last1=Farrelly |first1=Helen |title=Met Open Heads to the Meadow Brook Club |url=https://mgagolf.org/met-open-at-meadow-brook-club-preview/ |date=13 August 2025 |access-date=17 February 2026 |archive-date=19 August 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250819201844/https://mgagolf.org/met-open-at-meadow-brook-club-preview/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

Just like professional golfer [[Bobby Jones (golfer)|Bobby Jones]], [[Joyce Wethered]] was considered to be a star in the 1920s.<ref name=":1">{{Cite news |url=https://womensgolfjournal.com/golf/no-women-allowed/ |title=Gentlemen Only, Ladies Forbidden – a history |date=11 April 2016 |work=Women's Golf Journal |access-date=21 December 2017 |archive-date=22 December 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171222053121/https://womensgolfjournal.com/golf/no-women-allowed/ |url-status=dead }}</ref> Jones praised Wethered in 1930 after they had played an exhibition against each other. He doubted that there had ever been a better golfer, man or woman.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1997/11/25/sports/joyce-wethered-96-top-golfer-of-the-20-s.html |title=Joyce Wethered, 96, Top Golfer of the 20's |last=Litsky |first=Frank |date=25 November 1997 |work=The New York Times |access-date=21 December 2017 |issn=0362-4331 |archive-date=22 December 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171222105613/http://www.nytimes.com/1997/11/25/sports/joyce-wethered-96-top-golfer-of-the-20-s.html |url-status=live }}</ref>

The [[Royal Liverpool Golf Club|Royal Liverpool's]] club refused entry of [[Henry Cotton (golfer)|Sir Henry Cotton]]'s wife into the clubhouse in the late 1940s. The secretary of the club released a statement saying, "No woman ever has entered the clubhouse and, praise God, no woman ever will."<ref name=":1" /> The following year [[Babe Didrikson Zaharias|Babe Zaharias]] became the first woman to attempt to qualify for the [[U.S. Open (golf)|U.S. Open]], but her application was rejected by the [[United States Golf Association|USGA]]. They stated that the event was intended to be open to men only.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=K01hAAAAIBAJ&pg=2239,1001149&dq=los-angeles+zaharias+golf&hl=en |title=Men Only—Answer To Mrs. Zaharias Golf Entry |newspaper=The Telegraph-Herald |first=Ben |last=Phlegar |agency=Associated Press |date=7 April 1948 |page=11 |location=Dubuque, Iowa |via=news.google.com |access-date=21 December 2017 |archive-date=29 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211029223152/https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=K01hAAAAIBAJ&pg=2239%2C1001149&dq=los-angeles+zaharias+golf&hl=en |url-status=live }}</ref>

The [[LPGA|Ladies Professional Golf Association]] was formed in 1950 as a way to popularize the sport and provide competitive opportunities for golfers.<ref name=":1" /> In 1972 [[United States Congress|U.S. Congress]] passed the [[Title IX|Title IX of the Education Amendments]], which helped golf have more equal opportunities.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.justice.gov/crt/overview-title-ix-education-amendments-1972-20-usc-1681-et-seq |title=Overview Of Title IX Of The Education Amendments Of 1972, 20 U.S.C. A§ 1681 Et. Seq. |publisher=Department of Justice |access-date=21 December 2017 |archive-date=26 February 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160226180003/http://www.justice.gov/crt/overview-title-ix-education-amendments-1972-20-usc-1681-et-seq |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Marksbury |first=Jessica |date=December 19, 2022 |title=Title Wave |url=https://www.usga.org/content/usga/home-page/articles/2022/12/title-wave.html |website=USGA.org |access-date=16 February 2026 |archive-date=10 February 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260210144309/https://www.usga.org/content/usga/home-page/articles/2022/12/title-wave.html |url-status=live }}</ref> American [[Renee Powell]] moved to the UK in the 1970s to further her career, and became the first woman to play in a British men's tournament in 1977.<ref name=hennessy>{{cite news|first=John|last=Hennessy|title=Golf|newspaper=The Times|location=London|date=7 September 1977|page=8|access-date=18 September 2018|url=http://find.galegroup.com/ttda/infomark.do?&source=gale&prodId=TTDA&userGroupName=kccl&tabID=T003&docPage=article&searchType=BasicSearchForm&docId=CS134578471&type=multipage&contentSet=LTO&version=1.0|url-access=subscription }}</ref>

As of 2025, there is still a significant pay gap in women's golf such as the [[LPGA]] and the [[Ladies European Tour]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Stephen |first=Iona |date=2025-07-03 |title=Why the gender pay gap in golf still matters |url=https://www.todays-golfer.com/news-and-events/opinion/iona-stephen-why-the-gender-pay-gap-in-golf-still-matters/ |access-date=2026-02-16 |website=Today&#39;s Golfer |language=en |archive-date=17 February 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260217000310/https://www.todays-golfer.com/news-and-events/opinion/iona-stephen-why-the-gender-pay-gap-in-golf-still-matters/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Golf is still one of the sports with the most pay disparity by sex,<ref>{{Cite web |title=BBC Audio {{!}} Business Daily {{!}} What's behind golf's gender pay gap? |url=https://www.bbc.com/audio/play/w3ct5zn1 |access-date=2026-02-16 |website=www.bbc.com |language=en-GB |archive-date=15 November 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251115204927/https://www.bbc.com/audio/play/w3ct5zn1 |url-status=live }}</ref> and is also one that is physically demanding for women.<ref>{{cite journal | last1=Villanen | first1=Jemina | last2=Pedersen | first2=Arve Vorland | title=Relative golf performance between men and women: A comparison of absolute differences in performance between players on the PGA and LPGA tours considering physiological and anthropometric sex-based differences | journal=PLOS ONE | date=2026 | volume=21 | issue=5 | article-number=e0323074 | doi=10.1371/journal.pone.0323074 | doi-access=free | pmid=42113801 | pmc=13160328 | bibcode=2026PLoSO..2123074V }}</ref>

== Video games ==
{{Main|List of golf video games}}

Golf has been adapted and simulated in [[video games]]. [[Nintendo]]'s [[Golf (1984 video game)|''Golf'']] (1984) is recognized as the best-selling golf video game in history.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.guinnessworldrecords.com/world-records/98751-best-selling-golf-videogame |title=Best-selling golf videogame |publisher=Guinness World Records |access-date=4 May 2025 |archive-date=11 December 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241211213627/https://www.guinnessworldrecords.com/world-records/98751-best-selling-golf-videogame |url-status=live }}</ref> Other popular golf games include [[Access Software]]'s ''[[Leader Board]]'' and [[Links (series)|''Links'']] series, [[EA Sports]]'s [[PGA Tour (video game series)|''PGA Tour'']] series, and [[Sony Interactive Entertainment]]'s ''[[Everybody's Golf]]'' (aka ''Hot Shots Golf'').<ref>{{Cite web |last=Barr |first=Jeff |date=2023-03-17 |title=EA Sports re-emerges in golf video game space |url=https://www.globalgolfpost.com/biz/ea-sports-reemerges-in-golf-video-game-space/ |access-date=2024-12-06 |website=Global Golf Post |language=en-US |archive-date=7 December 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241207004852/https://www.globalgolfpost.com/biz/ea-sports-reemerges-in-golf-video-game-space/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2022-09-22 |title=Review: Easy Come Easy Golf (Switch) - Everybody's Golf Spiritual Successor Scores A Birdie On Switch |url=https://www.nintendolife.com/reviews/switch-eshop/easy-come-easy-golf |access-date=2024-12-06 |website=Nintendo Life |language=en-GB |archive-date=6 December 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241206235257/https://www.nintendolife.com/reviews/switch-eshop/easy-come-easy-golf |url-status=live }}</ref>

